export const body = "<p>In more ways than one, game environments resemble a collection of short stories. Each location is a tale untold, every turn and corner a life still living or used to live. These short stories are bound together by a thematic through-line that is the game's overarching narrative. In this metaphor, players are readers. When they pick up a new game, they expect to live a new life—a life in which they connect to many other lives through tracing the places those lives occupied or left behind. A life that might have been lived to the fullest, a life squandered in a moment of hesitation, a life undone by lofty ambitions. Sometimes, we learn the truth; other times, we ponder endlessly on the fragments that refuse to vanish, signs of something that once existed.</p>\n\n<p>Stories turn environments from mere real estate into lived-in spaces. Stories speak to our primal curiosity, suspend our disbelief, transport us to another realm. Environmental storytelling is, for the reasons listed, a vital ingredient for building captivating game levels.</p>\n\n<div class=\"source-panel\"></div>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/3b3072ecc45a7a2e3d16.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/3b3072ecc45a7a2e3d16.webp\" width=\"846\" height=\"476\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 1\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/aa4555b943d8d73c40ce.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/aa4555b943d8d73c40ce.webp\" width=\"1069\" height=\"602\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 2\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/5b40edeeb8490458e86b.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/5b40edeeb8490458e86b.webp\" width=\"1035\" height=\"582\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 3\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><em>The enemies in Borderlands 3 set up makeshift resting corners to enjoy their ever-interrupted respite.</em></p>\n\n<p><em><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/7d124b37d88666b48540.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/7d124b37d88666b48540.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 4\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></em></p>\n\n<p><em>The pirates in Chapter 14 of</em> Uncharted 4<em> must've had a ghastly final feast.</em></p>\n\n<h2>Enhance immersion</h2>\n\n<p>To be immersed means to be lost in a game, unknowing of the immediate reality around the game console. Adding history to your game world enriches its setting, which in turn keeps your players engaged. Ideally, players will habitually survey their surroundings, instead of ignoring anything that doesn’t advance their progression. But this is a balancing act, for we don’t want to overwhelm players with abundant information, nor do we want them to ceaselessly breeze through every square of the level without ever stopping.</p>\n\n<p>An illustrious application of environmental immersion is <em>BioShock</em>. Taking place in the underwater city Rapture, <em>BioShock</em> depicts the deplorable downfall of a society plagued by unrealistic philosophical ideals. Fabulous bars, lavish apartment complexes, and festive party venues are now corpse-ridden and blood-stained. Not a word spoken, but we still grasp what came before and what is coming.</p>\n\n<p>The sequel carries on this tradition. In <em>BioShock 2</em>, the player assumes the role of a Big Daddy, a formidable foe in the first game. As the name implies, Big Daddy has a daughter to care for, and despite his terrifying role in the last game, the sight of his daughter gauging her height still pulls at our heartstrings.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9ca32f557c08e8310c43.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9ca32f557c08e8310c43.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 5\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/d2082d5fd866efb757ef.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/d2082d5fd866efb757ef.webp\" width=\"1042\" height=\"586\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 6\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/fa00ed597c41a4d3f4bc.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/fa00ed597c41a4d3f4bc.webp\" width=\"963\" height=\"541\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 7\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/769aa2964bb0aa96be56.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/769aa2964bb0aa96be56.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 8\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Warn players of what to come</h2>\n\n<p>While environmental storytelling isn't exclusive to games, it benefits greatly from the interactive nature of this medium. Used properly, this technique can synthesize gameplay and story elements. A common use case is to warn players of incoming dangers. <em>The Last of Us</em> duology paint plenty of text on walls to simultaneously depict its degraded society and what ill intent is lurking around.<br></p>\n\n<p><em>Grounded</em> places players in the shoes of four shrunk teenagers, who justifiably embark on a quest to grow up. As small as bugs, they have to survive the precarious backyard where once harmless insects are now apex predators. An ant is now the size of a lion, a bee the size of a bear, and spiders appear as if they come from a sci-fi horror movie set. However, none of them is comparable to the Broodmother. Entering her lair, you are greeted by web upon web of the deceased insects, who unfortunately crawled into her territory. The creatures that give you trouble are just her repast. The scenery is a warning statement for what peril awaits inside.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/76d0a3acf6197f7b005d.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/76d0a3acf6197f7b005d.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 9\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/53e81c1a16469618ffa8.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/53e81c1a16469618ffa8.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 10\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/9d3a649291d470c42830.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/9d3a649291d470c42830.webp\" width=\"909\" height=\"569\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 11\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<h2>Teach a gameplay mechanic</h2>\n\n<p>Expanding on the latest example, environmental storytelling also doubles as an instructor, who can teach gameplay mechanics where appropriate. The quintessential example of this function is <em>Dead Space</em> with the line “Cut Their Limbs” written in blood. Though blatant, it fits the context and tone of the game.</p>\n\n<p>A more subtle example is <em>Half-Life 2</em>, which always primes players for nearby threats with equally gruesome clues. A scorched body hanging on an electrified fence is a sight of horror, but it's also a sign of environmental hazard. A raven abruptly swallowed by a Barnacle sticking to a ceiling not only sends shivers down our spines, but also a hint to circumvent their presence.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/416928a4e2bbdd011b47.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/416928a4e2bbdd011b47.webp\" width=\"1087\" height=\"815\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 12\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/5a09d42530d8d3c72b89.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/5a09d42530d8d3c72b89.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 13\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/fbe9c6810bfd2d7e48ec.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/fbe9c6810bfd2d7e48ec.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"646\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 14\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/a8906c39a7cbd8076579.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/a8906c39a7cbd8076579.webp\" width=\"1123\" height=\"631\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 15\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>\n\n<p>Similar to composition techniques, a piece of environmental storytelling can strike multiple goals at once. For example, <em>Bloodborne</em> opens with the player waking up in a clinic, then immediately thrown into a fight with a werewolf. Pushing past this confrontation, the player will make it to the streets of Central Yhanarm, where they see carcasses of insidious beasts on burning stakes. On the surface level, these stakes serve as set dressing elements, but if they're willing to experiment, they'll learn that fire counters most monsters in the area.</p>\n\n<p>Our final example is The Undead Settlement in <em>Dark Souls</em> <em>3</em>, which has a path littered with giant arrows. Once players get near this area, arrows will rain from the sky. Turns out, there is a giant sniping them from afar. This detail communicates both gameplay and story in a befitting manner for the level.</p>\n\n<p>As graphics get more realistic and the demand for emotionally resonant stories grow more significant, environmental storytelling is an aspect of game development no one can overlook. While cinematics deliver the overarching narrative, short stories woven into the polygons with which players interact flesh out the space they tread, and keep them engrossed. These tiny narrative threads, at full length or cut short, pull us into other worlds, other minds, other dreams. They are the paths we can take to reach the secluded side of the game universe and still be back in time for the main quest.</p>\n\n<p><a class=\"source-figure\" href=\"assets/concept-inline/34718eeff566fd6baa08.webp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener noreferrer\"><img src=\"assets/concept-inline/34718eeff566fd6baa08.webp\" width=\"1150\" height=\"647\" style=\"--source-width:566px\" alt=\"Illustration 16\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\"></a></p>";
